\f0\fs24 \cf2 In the museum's archives lies a small treasure: a watercolor drawing, pencil, and black ink signed by the Bagatti Valsecchi brothers themselves. This fascinating document allows us to see through their eyes two of the armors displayed in the gallery.\
On the left, a "fake antique" armor, crafted in Milan in the 19th century but inspired by late 16th-century models. At first glance, it seems perfect, but then subtle details reveal its true era: the symmetrical shoulder pieces, unrealistic for battle use, and the helmet with modern lines. These small inconsistencies remind us how the Renaissance, for the brothers, was more of a dream to evoke than a period to be reconstructed with historical accuracy.\
Below, a foot soldier's corslet, taken from an authentic 16th-century armor forged in Nuremberg. Less spectacular but much more authentic: there\'92s no leg protection because it was made for soldiers who fought quickly on foot, not on horseback.\
Two styles, two eras, but one great passion: for armor, sculpted metals, and the stories of battles fought and to be told.}